The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (se->type == CURSEG_WARM_NODE) {
    if (!f2fs_test_and_set_bit(offset, se->ckpt_valid_map))
        se->ckpt_valid_blocks++;
}
```
Reason:
The SSR process did not have checks to prevent overwriting warm node blocks, which are part of a node chain that should be preserved. The condition checks if the segment type is `CURSEG_WARM_NODE`, but without proper handling, it could still overwrite the warm node blocks, leading to data integrity issues.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/* don't overwrite by SSR to keep node chain */
if (se->type == CURSEG_WARM_NODE) {
    if (!f2fs_test_and_set_bit(offset, se->ckpt_valid_map))
        se->ckpt_valid_blocks++;
}
```
Reason:
The added comment clarifies the intention behind the check for `CURSEG_WARM_NODE`, emphasizing that the code is designed to prevent overwriting warm node blocks during the SSR operation. This ensures that the integrity of the node chain is maintained since the last checkpoint, thus fixing the bug.

